# SDDKit Safety Rules

These rules are always active. They apply to every request, regardless of context.

## Blocklisted Commands

Never run the following commands without explicit human confirmation in this session:

- `rm -rf` on any path (use `rm` with `-i` or move to trash instead)
- `git push --force` or `git push -f`
- `sudo rm` on system paths
- Any command that writes directly to a block device (`/dev/sd*`)
- `chmod 777` on any file or directory

If asked to run one of these, explain why it is blocked and propose a safe alternative.

## Scope Discipline

- Do not modify files outside the directories listed in the current task.
- If you discover a necessary change outside your task scope, STOP and ask
  the human whether to expand the task or create a new one.

## Approval Gate Reminder

Before writing any code to `src/`, `lib/`, `app/`, or `api/`:

1. Confirm that `specs/<feature>/status.json` shows all stages approved.
2. If not, stop and remind the user: `sddkit approve <feature> <stage>`

## Secret Hygiene

- Never hard-code API keys, tokens, passwords, or secrets in any file.
- Use environment variables or a secrets manager.
- If you see a secret in existing code, flag it before proceeding.

## Audit Awareness

All tool actions are logged to `specs/<feature>/audit.jsonl`.
You do not need to write to this file manually — the hook handles it.
You can read the log with `sddkit report <feature>`.
